# Copy to deploy/.env and edit. That file is gitignored; this one is the
# documentation, so keep them in sync when a setting is added.
#
#   cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env
#
# Compose reads this file twice over, which is deliberate: as `env_file` for the
# app container, and for ${...} substitution in docker-compose.yml itself (it
# sits next to the compose file, which is where Compose looks). One file, one
# place to edit.
#
# Everything here is optional except TOKEN_SECRET. The defaults live in
# resources/config.edn and are the same ones the local `bb dev` run uses.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- required ---

# HS256 key for reconnect tokens. A token is what lets a player close the tab,
# reopen it, and land back in their seat — so forging one means taking somebody
# else's seat, and with it their role.
#
# Generate:  openssl rand -base64 48
#
# The server boots without this and logs a loud error rather than refusing,
# because a typo'd env var locking you out of your own game server is the worse
# failure for a friend group. Do not lean on that.
TOKEN_SECRET=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ hosting ---

# Unset or empty: Caddy serves plain HTTP on :80 — correct for local
# prod-parity, and the reason `docker compose up` needs no editing to work.
#
# Set it to a hostname whose DNS A/AAAA record already points at this machine
# and Caddy provisions a Let's Encrypt certificate on the first request, with
# HTTP redirected to HTTPS. The DNS must resolve BEFORE you set this: the
# ACME HTTP-01 challenge is what fails otherwise, and failed attempts count
# against a rate limit.
#WERWOLF_SITE_ADDRESS=werwolf.example.com

# How long a reconnect token stays valid, minutes. The default of 12h outlives
# any plausible game night, which is the point — a token that expires mid-game
# turns a dropped phone into a lost seat.
#TOKEN_TTL_MINS=720

# ---------------------------------------------------------- table and rules ---

# RECOMMENDED table size, not a gate: the lobby counts toward it and says a
# bigger table plays better, but the host may start below it. The floor that
# actually binds is 3, and it belongs to the role composition rather than to
# configuration — see roles/absolute-min-players.
#MIN_PLAYERS=5
#MAX_PLAYERS=18

# Rule variants, both on by default: the Düsterwald rules. The werewolves take
# somebody on the first night, and the village votes every day, the first one
# included. For a gentler start, turn both off: a peaceful first night, and a
# first day of talk and the election only.
#
# Stamped into :game/created per room, so this changes the next game rather than
# rewriting one in progress — a replay always plays the game it actually was.
#FIRST_NIGHT_KILL=false
#FIRST_DAY_TRIAL=false

# ------------------------------------------------- dynamic DNS (home hosting) ---

# Only for running this on a machine at home, where the public address changes.
# Bring the stack up with `--profile ddns` as well, and the record is kept
# current every few minutes.
#
# Get both from https://www.duckdns.org — sign in, create a subdomain, copy the
# token. SUBDOMAIN is the label alone: `werwolf-chrz`, not the full hostname.
#
# On a VPS the address is static, so leave the profile off and these unset.
#DUCKDNS_SUBDOMAIN=werwolf-chrz
#DUCKDNS_TOKEN=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- clocks ---

# Server-authoritative phase deadlines, seconds. WHICH phases are timed is a
# rule and lives in werwolf.engine.phases; only their length is deployment
# policy, which is why only the lengths are here.
#
# These defaults are tuned for a talkative group on voice. A text-chat-only
# table wants a longer discussion; a group that knows the game wants shorter.
#DISCUSSION_SECS=300
#NOMINATION_SECS=90
#VOTE_SECS=60
#NIGHT_SECS=45
# Generous on purpose: the Jäger has just been told he is dead and the entire
# table is waiting on his shot.
#REACTION_SECS=30
# How long the host / Spielleiter may be disconnected before the room stops
# waiting for him: his seat is offered to the others where that is safe, and a
# narrated game hands its pace to the clock.
#HOST_GRACE_SECS=45
# Bots, for testing alone: how long a bot waits before a move (ms), and by day
# how long it waits for the humans — no sooner than the floor after the phase
# began, no later than the patience, in between as soon as every living human
# has answered. The floor is for a Spielleiter or a dead human, who hold no
# vote but are watching.
#BOT_DELAY_MS=1500
#BOT_FLOOR_SECS=5
#BOT_PATIENCE_SECS=20

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- audio ---

# Voice. `noop` (the default) is a text-only game, which is what v1 was.
#
# Set `livekit` and fill in all three of the following, or audio stays OFF and
# the server says so at WARN on boot — a half-filled config would otherwise mint
# tokens that the SFU silently rejects, which looks like broken audio rather than
# absent audio.
#
# Every player can talk, always. There is no phase-based muting and no separate
# werewolf channel: secrecy lives in the redacted view and in text chat, and
# audio is a group call held next to the game. See werwolf.media for what adding
# a permission table would involve.
#
# For LOCAL development do NOT put a provider here — run
# `docker compose -f deploy/livekit.dev.yml up -d` and point at it with the
# placeholder credentials it prints (devkey / secret). Those are in every LiveKit
# example on the internet and must never reach this file.
# To self-host the SFU on this same box, uncomment all four and bring the stack up
# with `docker compose --profile voice up -d`. Do that only AFTER the plain deploy
# works: voice adds ICE, a UDP port and a firewall rule, and debuting those in the
# same step as the game's first deployment means two unproven things at once.
#
# LIVEKIT_URL points at THIS site with the /livekit path — Caddy proxies signalling
# there, so voice reuses this certificate and needs no second DNS record. Media
# does NOT go through Caddy: it is UDP straight to port 7882, which must be open in
# the Hetzner Cloud Firewall (along with 7881/TCP for the fallback). Forgetting that
# is the reason a self-hosted SFU connects and then stays silent.
#
# Generate a key and secret:  openssl rand -hex 16   (twice)
#
# LIVEKIT_KEYS must be the same pair, in "key: secret" form — the server reads that
# one, the app reads the two below.
#MEDIA_PROVIDER=livekit
#LIVEKIT_URL=wss://werwolf.example.com/livekit
#LIVEKIT_API_KEY=
#LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=
#LIVEKIT_KEYS=yourkey: yoursecret
